    "evidence_text": "Stochastic Answer Networks for SQuAD 2.0\tThis paper presents an extension of the Stochastic Answer Network (SAN), one of the state-of-the-art machine reading comprehension models, to be able to judge whether a question is unanswerable or not.\n\nThe extended SAN contains two components: a span detector and a binary classifier for judging whether the question is unanswerable, and both components are jointly optimized.\n\nExperiments show that SAN achieves the results competitive to the state-of-the-art on Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD) 2.0.\n\nTo facilitate the research on this field, we release our code: https://github.com/kevinduh/san_mrc.\n\nmany existing MRC systems  ,  ,  ,  ,  , which extract answers by finding a sub-string in the passages/paragraphs, we propose a model that not only extracts answers but also predicts whether such an answer should exist.\n\nUsing a multi-task learning approach (c.f.  ), we extend the Stochastic Answer Network (SAN)  for MRC answer span detector to include a classifier that whether the question is unanswerable.\n\nThe two models share the same lower layer to save the number of parameters, and separate the top layers for different tasks (the span detector and binary classifier).\n\nModel\tThe Machine Reading Comprehension is a task which takes a question INLINEFORM0 and a passage/paragraph INLINEFORM1 as inputs, and aims to find an answer span INLINEFORM2 in INLINEFORM3 .\n\nOur model is a variation of SAN  , as shown in Figure FIGREF2 .\n\nThe main difference is the additional binary classifier added in the model justifying whether the question is unanswerable.\n\nThe shared layer is almost identical to the lower layers of SAN, which has a lexicon encoding layer, a contextual layer and a memory generation layer.\n\nanswer module for the span detector and a one-layer feed forward neural network for the binary classification task.\n\nWe map the symbolic/surface feature of INLINEFORM0 and INLINEF